This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

### Version Used

`main` at b907b90240f, through `RazorFormatter.TryFormatAsync` (the `dotnet format` entry point), with `indent_size = 4`, the default `razor_attribute_indent_style` (`align_with_first`) and the shared test project's imports. It reproduces the same way on the head of #86434 (d289a7e5a0e).

### Steps to Reproduce

Format this `.razor` file, then format the result again a few times:

```razor
<button foo="bar"
        @onclick="() =>
        {
            StateHasChanged();
        }">
</button>
```

### Expected Behavior

Each pass produces the same output as the one before it.

### Actual Behavior

The block lambda body moves 4 columns to the right on every pass.

Pass 1:

```razor
<button foo="bar"
        @onclick="() =>
            {
                StateHasChanged();
            }">
</button>
```

Pass 2:

```razor
<button foo="bar"
        @onclick="() =>
                {
                    StateHasChanged();
                }">
</button>
```

Pass 4:

```razor
<button foo="bar"
        @onclick="() =>
                        {
                            StateHasChanged();
                        }">
</button>
```

#86434 fixes the same growth when the lambda attribute is on the start tag's first line, for example `<button @onclick="() =>`. It doesn't fix it here, where the attribute is on a continuation line of a multi-line start tag. The shared formatting tests have 4 inputs of this shape: `<button foo="bar"` / `@onclick="()=>{` / `StateHasChanged();}">`, with and without an enclosing `<div>`, and with `() =>` / `{` on separate lines. All 4 are still not idempotent on #86434's branch.

### Version Used

`main` at b907b90240f, Visual Studio (cohost). Left out of #86307, which made `.editorconfig` `indent_size` work; this is the Tools > Options side.

Not reproduced in Visual Studio itself, which I can't run. Found by reading the code, and confirmed with a unit test of `RazorLSPTextViewConnectionListener` (see below).

### Steps to Reproduce

1. In Tools > Options > Text Editor > Razor (ASP.NET Core) > Tabs, set Tab size 4, Indent size 2, Insert spaces.
2. With no `.editorconfig` setting `indent_size`, format this document:

```razor
<div>
<span>Content</span>
</div>
@code {
void M()
{
}
}
```

### Expected Behavior

Indented by 2, the Indent size:

```razor
<div>
  <span>Content</span>
</div>
@code {
  void M()
  {
  }
}
```

### Actual Behavior

Indented by 4, the Tab size:

```razor
<div>
    <span>Content</span>
</div>
@code {
    void M()
    {
    }
}
```

`RazorLSPTextViewConnectionListener.UpdateRazorEditorOptions` reads `LANGPREFERENCES3.uTabSize` and never `uIndentSize` (nothing in the repo reads `uIndentSize`), and stores the tab size in `ClientSpaceSettings.IndentSize`. The LSP formatting request only carries `TabSize`, so `GetRazorFormattingOptions` falls back to the tab size when `.editorconfig` has no `indent_size`. Code actions and the edit service, which format without a request, use `ClientSpaceSettings.IndentSize`, which is also the tab size.

On `main`, `RazorLSPTextViewConnectionListenerTest` with `uIndentSize = 2` added next to `uTabSize = 8` still passes, and its strict mock expects `Update(new ClientSpaceSettings(IndentWithTabs: true, 8))`, so the indent size is dropped ([run on my fork](https://github.com/Mpdreamz/roslyn/actions/runs/43187175418), net472).

### Version Used

`main` at b907b90240f, through `RazorFormatter.TryFormatAsync` (the `dotnet format` entry point), with `indent_size = 4` and the shared test project's imports. Found while working on #86428.

### Steps to Reproduce

Format this `.razor` file, then format the result again:

```razor
@if (true)
{
    <script>
        var x = 1;
        function f() {
        }
    </script>
}
```

### Expected Behavior

Unchanged.

### Actual Behavior

First pass:

```razor
@if (true)
{
    <script>
            var x = 1;
            function f() {
            }
    </script>
}
```

Second pass, on the output of the first:

```razor
@if (true)
{
    <script>
                var x = 1;
                function f() {
                }
    </script>
}
```

Each pass indents the script body one more level, so the formatter isn't idempotent. `razor_ignore_indentation_in_script_or_style_blocks_with_complex_razor` doesn't change the result: the output is the same with `true` and `false`. The shape of the end tag doesn't matter either. `<style>` behaves the same way, and each enclosing C# block adds one level per pass. With a `<style>` inside `@if` inside `@foreach`, the body moves two levels per pass.

The IDE path isn't affected. There, the generated C# document takes the script body's indentation from the Html formatter's output. The Html formatter leaves elements inside `@if` at column 0, so `CSharpDocumentGenerator` only has to subtract the enclosing Html elements (`GetHtmlIndentationLevel`). `dotnet format` doesn't run an Html formatter, so the indentation comes straight from the source. That source indentation already includes the `@if` level, and nothing subtracts it.
